# Bit Foundry

Bit Foundry is the first lesson because it starts with accumulation before interpretation. The
player taps, buys multipliers, watches a hidden machine become visible, and eventually meets The
Defragmenter: a routine that is not stronger through skill, only through an unfair flag.

The important tool is the raw editor. `Overwriter.frag` looks like noise, but it is still a file,
and files can be inspected. Changing or removing the `CHEAT` line disables the unfair routine for
this save. The boss is not defeated by that edit. It only becomes possible to fight fairly.

That distinction matters for the rest of Defragmenter. The file viewer does not act as a cheat
menu. It gives evidence, switches, views, comparisons, searches, and metadata that make an
impossible situation understandable.

Narratively this is genesis: the entity learns that its environment has rules, and that some rules
are written down in places a user can reach. The Defragmenter is not a cartoon enemy. It is a
process optimizing away disorder, even when the disorder is the player.

Next, Glyph Dungeon changes the shape of the lesson. Instead of editing a visible line, the player
has to search a long file for the one marked passage that makes an ambiguous pattern navigable.
